#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# spherical_plot3d

#% text
# `spherical_plot3d(r, [theta, a, b], [phi, c, d])` draws the surface at distance r from the origin, at polar angle theta (from the z axis) and azimuth phi.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# r may depend on theta and phi; theta usually runs from 0 to pi and phi from 0 to 2 pi.

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG with SVG and PDF copies next to it.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The unit sphere:

#% cell
spherical_plot3d(1, [t, 0, pi], [p, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("spherical_plot3d.files/spherical_plot3d-1.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A bumpy sphere:

#% cell
spherical_plot3d(1 + sin(5t)*sin(5p)/5, [t, 0, pi], [p, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("spherical_plot3d.files/spherical_plot3d-2.png")

#% text
# Part of a sphere:

#% cell
spherical_plot3d(1, [t, 0, pi/2], [p, 0, 3pi/2])
#% output
#= image("spherical_plot3d.files/spherical_plot3d-3.png")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Without mesh lines, coloured by height:

#% cell
spherical_plot3d(1 + cos(t)^2, [t, 0, pi], [p, 0, 2pi], mesh: none, color_function: ocean)
#% output
#= image("spherical_plot3d.files/spherical_plot3d-4.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `parametric_plot3d`, `plot3d`, `revolution_plot3d`
